@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+- **blocker** `scripts/whatif.py:172` — `load_metrics` still aggregates every `set_id`. ADR-0014 permits overlapping measurement sets, so this double-counts vehicle-time and can change A/B rankings. The new map and corridor tools reject this case, while their documentation incorrectly claims `whatif.py` does too.
+- **blocker** `scripts/whatif.py:482` — Different interval windows only emit a warning; the tool then performs t-tests, assigns `UPGRADE`/`WORSE`, and writes the invalid report. Non-comparable arms must fail closed before scoring.
+- **should-fix** `scripts/whatif.py:216` — The reported window starts at requested `warmup`, not the first retained interval boundary. With a mid-interval warmup, the report overstates the measurement window and cannot detect differing starts between arms. Track `min(begin_tick)` as the map does.
+- **should-fix** `scripts/chicago/corridorspeed.py:110` — Same inaccurate start label: it prints `args.warmup_tick` rather than the first included interval’s `begin_tick`.
+- **should-fix** `scripts/whatif.py:480` — Sorting windows can raise `TypeError` when one arm has no complete interval (`[warmup, None]`) and another has an integer end, instead of reporting the intended comparability failure.
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
